winebus: build the motors and do not write them, on request.

AN INSTRUMENT, NOT A FEATURE, and marked as one wherever it is named -- the
second in this series after mgvf-0008. It is OFF by default and a build
installed with nothing under the pad's key behaves exactly as before.

WHAT IT IS FOR. A DualSense on Bluetooth costs an Unreal title 3 to 5 frames a
second and drops its audio while the motors are being driven; the same pad on a
cable costs nothing. Five sessions of measurement have eliminated every
mechanism proposed for it. Not CPU: bluetoothd goes from 1.8% of a core to at
most 2.9% while the game runs at 200-420%, and winedevice.exe does not move.
Not the pad's input stream: delivery to the client is unchanged in the median
and rises only in the tail. Not a control-channel handshake: an output write on
an idle link costs 0.19 to 0.57 ms, where a real control transaction on the same
link costs 25 to 48 ms. Not Bluetooth audio: the system's output is the built-in
speakers and the pad is not an audio endpoint. Not sniff: 34,672 lines of
bluetoothd logging across a session carry no mode transition at all.

WHAT WAS NEVER SEPARATED. Turning the rumble off removes the write to the radio
AND the whole path the request takes through the guest to reach it: the game's
XInputSetState, hidclass's dispatch, the IOCTL that takes the device lock, the
event that round-trips wineserver, all of it on the game's own thread and all of
it under Rosetta. Every experiment so far has removed both at once and concluded
about one of them. This removes exactly one.

With XInputRumbleDryRun set, everything happens except the last step. The pad
does not buzz -- that is the point, and an ERR line says so at device arrival so
that a log cannot be misread later as a pad that failed.

WHAT IT ALSO SKIPS, because a measurement has to declare its own edges: on
Bluetooth the skipped branch would have translated the packet into a 0x31 and
signed it, so those go too. That is a few microseconds of arithmetic on the
haptics thread and nothing at all on the game's, so it cannot be what a frame
rate notices -- but it is skipped, and saying so is cheaper than someone
discovering it while distrusting the result.

HOW TO READ THE OUTCOME. If the frame rate and the audio recover, the cost is
the write, and what remains is choosing an interval against a link that carries
about 65 reports a second. If they do not, the radio was never the cause, the
guest-side path is, and a good deal of what has been proposed for this -- an
asynchronous write, a different transport, handing the pad to SDL -- would have
been work spent on the wrong half.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
@@ -133,6 +133,9 @@
     /* The owner's strength preference, for the transports where nothing
      * downstream applies it. 100 leaves what a game asked for alone. */
     unsigned int haptics_gain;
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0017: an INSTRUMENT, not a feature. Non-zero makes the haptics
+     * thread do everything it does except reach the radio. */
+    unsigned int haptics_dry_run;
     /* The motor write happens on this thread and not on the caller's. `wanted`
      * is the last thing asked for, packed as rumble<<16|buzz so that it can be
      * exchanged in one instruction without a lock. */
@@ -1864,7 +1867,21 @@
          * preferences are applied on the way -- so what a client asked of the
          * motors here is scaled by the same percentage as what a client asks
          * of them itself. */
-        if (ext->desc.bus_type == BUS_TYPE_BLUETOOTH)
+        /* MGVF mgvf-0017: and here is the one step the dry run skips.
+         *
+         * WHAT IT ALSO SKIPS, said plainly so the result is read correctly: on
+         * Bluetooth this branch would first translate the packet into a 0x31 and
+         * sign it, so a dry run spares those too. That is a few microseconds of
+         * arithmetic on THIS thread and nothing on the game's, so it cannot be
+         * what a frame rate notices -- but it is skipped, and a reader deserves
+         * to know rather than assume. Everything on the game's own thread is
+         * untouched. */
+        if (ext->haptics_dry_run)
+        {
+            io.Status = STATUS_SUCCESS;
+            io.Information = out.reportBufferLen;
+        }
+        else if (ext->desc.bus_type == BUS_TYPE_BLUETOOTH)
             dualsense_usb_set_output_report(device, &out, &io);
         else
             unix_device_set_output_report(device, &out, &io);
@@ -2036,6 +2053,22 @@
     if (ext->haptics_gain != 100 && ext->desc.bus_type != BUS_TYPE_BLUETOOTH)
         TRACE("device %p: what a game asks of the motors is scaled to %u%%\n", device, ext->haptics_gain);
 
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0017: the dry run. OFF unless asked for, and it exists to answer
+     * one question that five sessions of measurement could not: whether the cost
+     * a game pays for rumble on this pad is the WRITE TO THE RADIO or the path
+     * the request takes through the guest before it ever gets here.
+     *
+     * Turning XInputRumble off does not answer it, because that removes both at
+     * once -- which is what made the owner's control run inconclusive. With this
+     * set, the game still calls XInputSetState, hidclass still dispatches, the
+     * IOCTL still takes the device lock, the event still round-trips wineserver,
+     * this thread still wakes, still coalesces, still builds the packet. Only
+     * the last step does not happen. */
+    ext->haptics_dry_run = get_device_option(ext->desc.vid, ext->desc.pid, L"XInputRumbleDryRun", 0);
+    if (ext->haptics_dry_run)
+        ERR("device %p: XInputRumbleDryRun is set -- the motors will be built and NOT written. "
+            "This is a measurement, and the pad will not buzz.\n", device);
+
     ext->haptics_wanted = 0;
     ext->haptics_wake = CreateEventW(NULL, FALSE, FALSE, NULL);
     ext->haptics_stop = CreateEventW(NULL, TRUE, FALSE, NULL);
